%U A399719 6,3,5,4,3,3,8,8,1,9,0,2,4,2,6,1,2,9,2,6,1,5,9,1,2,7,3,2,8,4,0,7,7,1,0,8,3
%N A399719 Decimal expansion of the largest root of x^5 + x^4 - 7*x^3 - 7*x^2 + 9*x + 8.
%C A399719 Consider an algebraic number satisfying x = sqrt(a + sqrt(b + sqrt(c + sqrt(d + x)))), where a, b, c, d belongs to Q. It appears that if x is an algebraic number of degree 5, then the only solutions are: (3, 3, 4, 8), (3, 4, 8, 3), (4, 8, 3, 3), (8, 3, 3, 4).
%C A399719 Is there a longer minimal coefficient period for an infinitely nested quadratic radicals leading to a quintic algebraic number?
%C A399719 Does this quintic algebraic number belong to a larger family of quintic algebraic numbers with minimal coefficient periods of length 4? In case of minimal coefficient periods of length 3, the period [k^2 + k - 1, k^2 + k + 1, k^2 + 3*k + 2], for every positive integer k, leads to an algebraic number of degree at most 5.
%H A399719 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A399719/b399719.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399719 <a href="/index/Al#algebraic_05">Index entries for algebraic numbers, degree 5</a>
%F A399719 Equals sqrt(3 + sqrt(3 + sqrt(4 + sqrt(8 + ...)))), with coefficient period [3, 3, 4, 8].
%F A399719 The other roots of x^5 + x^4 - 7*x^3 - 7*x^2 + 9*x + 8 and their sign periods are:
%F A399719   sqrt(3 - sqrt(3 - sqrt(4 - sqrt(8 + ...)))), [+, -, -, -],
%F A399719   -sqrt(3 + sqrt(3 + sqrt(4 - sqrt(8 - ...)))), [-, +, +, -],
%F A399719   -sqrt(3 - sqrt(3 + sqrt(4 + sqrt(8 - ...)))), [-, -, +, +],
%F A399719   -sqrt(3 - sqrt(3 - sqrt(4 + sqrt(8 - ...)))), [-, -, -, +].
%e A399719 2.32044250444435302201555224873...
%t A399719 First[RealDigits[Root[#^5 + #^4 - 7*#^3 - 7*#^2 + 9*# + 8 &, 5], 10, 100]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 20 2026 *)
